# The Scientific Eye

One face and eight arms; a golden body clothed in golden silk and ornaments; incense, lotus, water vessel, bell, and rosary held in his hands. Seated upon a white lotus serving as the throne of a one-wheeled chariot, he starts from the left of Mount Meru, circles it, crosses the cities of Indra, Yama, Varuna, and Soma, and returns to Indra's city. The chariot has one wheel and seven horses.

When the Sun God sets forth in this splendour, Pulastya, Pulaha, Vasishtha, Angiras, Kausika, Bharadvaja, Bhrigu, Kratu, Gautama, Kasyapa, Jamadagni, and Atri attend in monthly rotation and sing praises.

The serpents Kankan, Takkan, Nagan, Kambalasvan, Niran, Airavatan, Elapatran, Karkotakan, Sankhapalan, Dhananjayan, Paraman, and Vasuki take turns by month pulling that single-wheeled chariot harnessed to seven horses.

Nor is that all. There is song and dance before the chariot. A succession of named celestial singers performs month by month, while beauties including Menaka, Ghritachi, Visvachi, Urvasi, Purvachitti, Tilottama, and Rambha dance.

Do not imagine that the Sun God neglected family affairs merely because he had the ceaseless daily task of circling Meru. He had two wives, one arising from the other. He took Sanjna Devi as wife and, amid all his duties, fathered Vaivasvata Manu, Yama, and Yamuna.

Love seems to have acted like a cooling breeze that enabled Sanjna to endure the Sun's heat until she bore three children. Later she could bear it no longer. She transformed her "shadow" into a woman, left it behind, and returned to her parental home. The Sun continued his amorous life with Chaya Devi without knowing she was another woman.

Only later did he discover that his companion was not Sanjna but Chaya. He went in search of his wife, who for some reason had assumed the form of a mare and was performing penance on a mountainside. Did the Sun ask why she wore that form and had left him? No. He immediately became a stallion and pursued her. The chase became a love game; seed flowed from the solar horse's nostrils, and the Asvini gods were born.

This is the sun of our country, the "truth" discovered by the finest rishis, ascetics, and Vedic scholars. With Aha and Uhu singing and Rambha and Urvasi dancing, the Sun God rides his one-wheeled chariot around Meru.

Those who gave us this truth are described not as ordinary people, nor merely masters of every art, but as pure beings who subdued the three impurities, discovered the primal source, and befriended God. They are said to have perceived with the eye of wisdom truths invisible to the fleshly eye. Worship of the Sun God and its ritual method were prescribed accordingly.

The sun seen by their wisdom-eye had one face and eight arms. Their spectacle was magnificent: seven horses and serpents pulled the chariot around Meru amid song and dance. They claimed to have seen it. People accepted the claim; believers were called noble and doubters reviled as atheists.

Now let us examine how the sun seen by the wisdom-eye appears to today's scientific eye. There are many reasons to do so. Foremost is that in our present life we receive far more help from those with the scientific eye than from those with the wisdom-eye.

The image of the Sun God claimed by the wisdom-seers is accepted only by ordinary people here. Nowhere else does anyone say that the sun has eight arms, one face, and two wives. Even here, schoolchildren do not see the sun shown by the wisdom-eye. They, like ordinary people throughout the world, see another sun.

The wisdom-eye sees a god circling in a chariot. The scientific eye sees neither god, chariot, nor wheel, but a celestial body called the sun. It does not see the sun circling Meru; it sees many bodies revolving around the sun.

In an age of confused thought, the wisdom-seers merely claimed to see. Scientific observers not only see what they describe but show it to others, not through spells visible only to the blessed, but with suitable instruments. If even an ordinary conservative here challenges, "Come, let me see whether the sun is as you say," the scientist can lead him to a telescope and show him the heavenly bodies.

No matter how one pleads, jokes, or challenges, asking to be shown even four of the eight arms or three of the seven horses, no Sankaracharya, monastic head, Vedantin, or religious advocate here can display that Sun God. The sovereign sun accompanied by celestial singers and dancers is superstition plated with splendour.

Could the world accept otherwise? If those who planted this superstition in common minds possessed self-respect, should they not expel the scientific sun from books and learned thought? Yet they cannot drive it even from the minds of their own children.

The sun is one body among those in space, surrounded by clouds of fire. Eight planets revolve around it: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune.

The source gives its circumference as more than 2.6 million miles and its diameter as more than 870,000 miles. Its expanse is said to be 1.25 million times the earth's, and its mass 1.3 million times greater. It is said to lie approximately 92,830,000 miles from Earth. It contains many spots, described as a thousand miles deep.

None of this knowledge came through Narada's grace. It resulted from continuous investigation pursued night and day, with each conclusion becoming a new beginning.

In his essay "Visvarupa Darisanam," Achariyar, the present Governor-General, wrote of scientists: "Everything has been systematically measured and tested. Modern science leaves nothing as mere supposition. A hypothesis is applied again to events and tested tenfold before anything is stated. That is the special character of physical science."

He further wrote that these matters were tested with carefully constructed instruments, using the subtleties of mathematics and great caution.

Achariyar calls them tested conclusions. There lies science's distinction. Scientists are not content to say merely, "I think so" or "It may be so," nor do they impose guesses upon the public. The eminent rishis did precisely that. They saw visions, imagination welled up, and they made conjectures. They conducted no further tests, did not call their ideas conjecture, but converted them into sayings of wisdom and imposed them upon people. Some threatened them with religion and curses; common people surrendered their reason.

Achariyar also wrote that physicists use telescopes, microscopes, spectroscopes, and photographic instruments more sensitive than the human eye to discover the secrets of immensely distant objects. They do not hurry, become deluded or arrogant, stubbornly insist that their own thought alone is true, or act with anger and hatred.

No one could describe the scientist more clearly. Examined closely, Achariyar not only praises scientists but indirectly mocks the wisdom-seers by contrast. His description points to the opposite traits of earlier claimants: haste, arrogance, obstinacy, and anger.

Statements made long ago from some inference were declared eternally unchangeable; doubt became sin, and doubters were threatened with divine curses. Reason was imprisoned.

Here we rose each morning, folded our hands to the sun, and said at evening that it fell into the mountain's mouth. We devised rituals for solar worship. Meanwhile scholars investigated celestial bodies. Today the learned world accepts their discoveries, not the sun of the wisdom-eye.

While our people calculated what ritual would remove solar affliction, how much fee should be paid, whether a serpent image should be fashioned in silver or gold, and how special benefit might be secured, scientists discovered that the sun is a body many times larger than Earth, does not circle Meru or travel among celestial cities, and is instead circled by Earth and other planets.

While our people folded their hands toward the sky and cried, "Lord, see the suffering I endure," scholars examined space through telescopes. They discovered that the moon is a borrower with no light of its own, shining only by the sun's light. We portrayed the moon according to our Purana as conducting an illicit affair with Tara and remained satisfied. Scholars instead began extensive lunar research and discovered truths both astonishing and useful.

While we read stories of the Sun God, Moon God, Earth Goddess, and Ocean King, believed them, and designed worship accordingly, thinkers in other countries refused to regard what they already knew as sufficient and laboured for more truth.

The wisdom-seers pointed to Arundhati and narrated her shining story, attaching dramatic tales to many stars. Scientists went beyond these stories. They discovered that not everything visible in the sky is a star: there are stars and planets. Stars twinkle while planets shine steadily; stars emit their own light, while planets reflect sunlight; and stars lie many thousands of miles beyond the region of the planets.

For solar and lunar eclipses, we told the Rahu-Ketu story: serpent-shaped evildoers occasionally try to seize and swallow the sun or moon. We prescribed worship to remove eclipse pollution, placed darbha grass in vessels, and paid fees to priests muttering beside tanks.

Scientists meanwhile developed telescopes, microscopes, and magnifying devices, connected astronomy with mathematics, explained the cause of eclipses, and disclosed further truths. Even our household children now study scientific explanation rather than the serpent-swallowing story.

No matter whether one worships at Ramana's feet, bows to Aurobindo, carries kavadi in the month of Adi, observes silence, or remains rigorously orthodox, one cannot rewrite even our national curriculum to say that eclipses result from the Rahu-Ketu incident caused by a quarrel in heaven. The world would laugh.

The inability to deny new truths, unwillingness to relinquish their benefits, and lack of courage to abandon old attachments combine to make even informed people here strange beings. Our government declares a holiday for an eclipse according to old belief while funding astronomical research because it cannot reject new truth.

While our people calculated how much sesame, horse gram, betel nut, betel leaf, and money were required to remove planetary affliction, scholars elsewhere counted stars. About six thousand were said to be visible to the eye and approximately five hundred thousand through telescopes. They not only counted but photographed them, and continued research in the belief that improved telescopes would reveal many more hundreds of thousands.

Our thirst for knowledge was satisfied by saying the earth was flat, borne by the thousand-headed Adisesha, and was a goddess adorned by patience. Scholars did not rest content with such stories. Through the scientific eye they saw neither the thousand-headed serpent nor the goddess it supported, but an earth constantly rotating upon itself and revolving around the sun.

The world accepted this as truth. However much we described the matted locks, ochre water pots, sacrifices, and yogic practices of ancestors who assigned attributes to Earth, it did not accept their "ancient truth." Even our fiercest orthodox believer would not dare reject the scientific account.

Scientists determined the distance between us and the sun. The source says Earth travels at seventy-two thousand miles an hour and that its weight, nature, speed, and path have been calculated.

Claims attributed to the wisdom-eye have become imagination, conjecture, or philosophy. In the world's forum of knowledge, only truths found and stated by the scientific eye are accepted. Elsewhere, knowing these new truths marks education, teaching them is true instruction, and seeking truths beyond them is the scholar's duty. Here, belief in old legend and conduct based upon it are treated not merely as wisdom but as the mark of devotion.

We give the world these two pieces of news:

1. Today the Government grants a holiday for a solar eclipse and arranges security for sea bathing.
2. Government observatory staff at Kodaikanal are studying a comet newly visible for several days.

When the learned world considers these two reports together, will it not begin studying the mental condition of our people? An observatory at Kodaikanal, yet sea bathing with government arrangements to remove eclipse pollution. Anyone would ask why we follow this peculiar course.

As these "strange-minded mystics," we enjoy the benefits obtained from truths discovered by the scientific eye while simultaneously believing and worshipping superstitions built upon conjectures attributed to the wisdom-eye that the modern forum of knowledge cannot approach. So long as we remain such strange beings, we cannot find a place in the world of knowledge.

(*Dravida Nadu*, 21 November 1948)

---NOTES---
`ஞானக்கண்` and `விஞ்ஞானக்கண்` are rendered consistently as `wisdom-eye` and `scientific eye` to retain the title's central contrast. The essay's astronomical numbers and its eight-planet list are preserved as historical source claims from 1948; they are not silently modernised. `ஆச்சாரியார்`, identified in the source as the then Governor-General, refers to C. Rajagopalachari (Rajaji). Several long celestial-name lists contain uncertain OCR spellings; recognisable names are transliterated conservatively and the argument does not depend on resolving every variant. The publication date was restored from the final scan.
